<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lü Bu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 199</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Lü Fengxian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lü Bu was a Chinese warlord of the late Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, famed for his martial skill and notorious for betraying his patrons. He killed Ding Yuan and then the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> in 192, later seized Xu Province and fought both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a>, who had proclaimed his own dynasty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>. Cao Cao captured and executed him at Xiapi in 199.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lu-bu.jpg" alt="Lü Bu" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%BC_Bu_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lü Bu came from Jiuyuan County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuyuan-commandery">Wuyuan Commandery</a>, on the northern frontier of the Han empire, near present-day Baotou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, and was known for his martial valour in Bing Province. No description of his appearance survives in historical records, but he specialised in archery and horse-riding, had great physical strength and was nicknamed the Flying General. He owned a steed called Red Hare, and a saying of the time ran: among men, Lü Bu; among steeds, Red Hare. Ding Yuan, appointed Inspector of Bing, recruited him as a registrar and treated him with exceptional kindness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 190 a coalition of warlords led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> marched against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a>, who had installed Emperor Xian as a puppet. Lü Bu defended Dong Zhuo, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian">Sun Jian</a> drove him back, and Dong burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. In 192 Lü Bu killed Dong Zhuo, at the instigation of Wang Yun and Shisun Rui; Dong's followers then attacked Chang'an, and Lü Bu fought a duel with Guo Si and eventually abandoned the city. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a>, who according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguozhi</a> refused him, while the Houhanshu says Yuan treated him generously until his arrogance and plundering worried him. He then helped Yuan Shao fight Zhang Yan, but Yuan too came to distrust him.</p><p>In 194, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> was attacking Xu Province, rebels invited Lü Bu into Yan Province, and he declared himself its governor. Cao took the territory back within two years. In 195 Lü Bu seized Xu Province from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, who had sheltered him. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a> declared himself emperor in early 197, Lü Bu, who had agreed to marry his daughter to Yuan's son, seized her back from the convoy and sent Yuan's envoy to Xu as a prisoner. Yuan attacked him in anger, and around late 197 or early 198 Lü Bu sided with Yuan Shu again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the winter of 198 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> besieged Xiapi. Lü Bu wanted to surrender, but Chen Gong and others, who had already offended Cao Cao, urged him not to. Captured and bound, he pleaded to be spared, and when Cao Cao hesitated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> reminded him what Lü Bu had done to Ding Yuan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a>. He was executed on 7 February 199. Chen Shou wrote that Lü Bu had the might of a tiger but lacked planning skills, and Fan Ye called him erratic and capricious. The 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> portrays him as a nearly invincible warrior but an incapable leader, and adds fictional stories such as his romance with Diaochan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lü Bu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Encouraged by Interior Minister Wang Yun, <b>Lü Bu</b>, sworn to Dong Zhuo as a son, struck the fatal blow at the palace gate in Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-22">22 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His usurpation lost him allies such as Sun Ce, and Cao Cao, <b>Lü Bu</b> and others turned against him.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhongjia">Zhongjia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jiuyuan District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Pizhou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.6,109.96&amp;year=189" aria-label="Jiuyuan District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lü Bu"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4351 4070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4351 4070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.6,109.96&amp;year=189">What was here in 189 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
